.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1206 AM PDT Sat Aug 8 2026
(Monday through Friday)

Global ensemble clusters are in agreement that upper-level shortwave
troughing will develop Monday off the West Coast between subtropical
high pressure across the Eastern Pacific Ocean and the Four Corners
high. This will result in temperatures moderating to near normal and
the return of a marine layer around 1,000 feet. Even with the
cooling trend and the deepening of the marine layer, the increase in
onshore wind will support elevated fire weather conditions into next
week. The upper-level pattern along with a resurgence in monsoonal
moisture may be enough to bring elevated rain showers/thunderstorms
to the forecast as early as Thursday and into next weekend. Whether
or not the aforementioned trough pinches off to become a low or not
will be important for the lifting mechanism factor. As far as
instability and moisture go, conditionally unstable lapse rates and
sufficient MUCAPE will overlap with PWAT values in excess of an
inch. While there`s no mention in the official forecast now, it may
be warranted as we get closer.

