.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1206 AM PDT Sat Aug 8 2026

Near-critical fire weather conditions persist through the weekend
across the interior due to very hot and dry conditions. Strong
thermal belting, extended burn periods, elevated PIG, and near or
record BI/ERC values are expected. Temperatures will be 10 to 15
degrees above normal with a compressed marine layer. Poor minimum
daytime relative humidity as low as 10% in the afternoons with
little-to-no recovery overnight is expected with nocturnal drying in
the highest terrain. Even with critically dry conditions, wind is
the limiting factor preventing this from being a Fire Weather
Watch/Red Flag Warning event. Outside of the diurnal sea breeze that
will bring breezy conditions in the afternoons and evenings through
gaps and passes, onshore flow will be light. Additionally, critical
conditions are not expected to meet coverage or duration
requirements to warrant a product. Elevated fire weather conditions
are expected into next week as onshore flow increases on the heels
of the near-critical hot and dry event this weekend. The upper-level
pattern along with a resurgence in monsoonal moisture may be enough
to bring elevated rain showers/thunderstorms to the forecast as
early as Thursday and into next weekend.

